      "updated": 1783972059392,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Streaming MVP plan:\n\n- httpServer:\n    - Use EventEmitter - https://nodejs.org/api/events.html\n    - create two new endpoints\n        - POST /webhooks/metrics\n            - start storing updated_at on metricsStore. \n                - If new timestamp is greater than current updated_at, emit event, and respond with 200\n                - Otherwise, skip emitting event, and respond with 200\n        - GET /api/metrics/stream\n            - listen to \"metrics.update\" event on internal eventEmitter instance.\n            - on \"metrics.update\" event, send the latest metrics as a chunk to the UI.\n            - res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream'); \\n\\n\\n\n- gRPCServer:\n    - after updating the metricsStore, send a POST /metrics/ request\n        - { event: \"metrics.update\", timestamp: \"2026-07-13T...\" }\n- ui:\n    - every 500 ms, try to initiate a stream via the GET /api/metrics/stream\n    - once initiated, continually listen for data, done events, updating state as I get data.\n    - SSE on the frontend uses EventSource\n   \n- Questions:\n    - What is the size limit of chunks I can send to the browser client over a stream? \n      Does this impose a limit to this \"send entirety of data on each send event and replace existing data with it each time\" approach?\n    - Are there different stream events I could utilize to break each payload up further on a stream if I hit that limit?\n    - My server is no longer state-less. The eventEmitter instance is shared between two endpoints.\n      How would you scale this to multiple server instances? \n      Can you store an eventEmitter instance elsewhere and emit/listen to it from two separate server instances? \n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Streaming MVP plan:\n\n- httpServer:\n    - Use EventEmitter - https://nodejs.org/api/events.html\n    - create two new endpoints\n        - POST /webhooks/metrics\n            - start storing updated_at on metricsStore. \n                - If new timestamp is greater than current updated_at, emit event, and respond with 200\n                - Otherwise, skip emitting event, and respond with 200\n        - GET /api/metrics/stream\n            - listen to \"metrics.update\" event on internal eventEmitter instance.\n            - on \"metrics.update\" event, send the latest metrics as a chunk to the UI.\n            - res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream'); \\n\\n\\n\n- gRPCServer:\n    - after updating the metricsStore, send a POST /metrics/ request\n        - { event: \"metrics.update\", timestamp: \"2026-07-13T...\" }\n- ui:\n    - every 500 ms, try to initiate a stream via the GET /api/metrics/stream\n    - once initiated, continually listen for data, done events, updating state as I get data.\n    - SSE on the frontend uses EventSource\n   \n- Questions:\n    - What is the size limit of chunks I can send to the browser client over a stream? \n      Does this impose a limit to this \"send entirety of data on each send event and replace existing data with it each time\" approach?\n    - Are there different stream events I could utilize to break each payload up further on a stream if I hit that limit?\n    - My server is no longer state-less. The eventEmitter instance is shared between two endpoints.\n      How would you scale this to multiple server instances? \n      Can you store an eventEmitter instance elsewhere and emit/listen to it from two separate server instances? \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
